WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.374
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.374 --> 00:00:04.360
I show how a new load
balancer protocol can

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:06.220
give a big performance boost.

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:08.022
Will we walk the
performance high wire?

00:00:08.022 --> 00:00:08.980
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.010
As we've seen before, Google
Cloud's networking suite offers

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:17.830
reliable, high performing
services for your cloud

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:21.460
applications, things like TCP
BBR, network service tiers,

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:23.620
load balancing, CDNs, DNS--

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:24.910
the list goes on.

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:27.850
The gist is that GCP's
networking services give you

00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:29.950
a lot of performance
with very little

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:31.630
heavy lifting from your team.

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:33.390
And that trend continues.

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:36.040
Recently, GCP's load
balancer added support

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:37.960
for the QUIC protocol.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:41.530
QUIC, or Quick UDP
Internet Connections,

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:43.660
is a modern transport
layer protocol

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:47.350
that provides congestion control
similar to TCP and security

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:51.560
equivalent to SSL, not to
mention improved performance.

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:54.280
Most of the magic of QUIC
is concentrated on reducing

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:56.920
the number of round trips
required when establishing

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:59.800
a new connection, including
the handshake step, encryption

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:02.050
step, and initial
data request, which

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:05.110
makes it a great option in areas
where you see less than ideal

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:07.060
connectivity from your clients.

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:10.570
To test it out, we first created
a simple HTTPS load balancer

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:13.960
that serves a 10-megabyte
file from a GCS bucket.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:16.510
Then we went into the
console and enabled the QUIC

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:19.330
negotiation setting for
the load balancer, which

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:22.180
allows the balancer to accept
QUIC connections from clients

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:25.420
that support it without
disturbing our existing HTTPS

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:26.080
users.

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:30.020
Now, it's worth noting that
much like TCP BBR, the benefits

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:32.110
of QUIC are best
seen in situations

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:34.150
where clients have
poor connectivity.

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:36.130
As such, to get a
true performance test,

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:38.080
we simulate a bad
network connection

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:42.820
using a TC command for both
our HTTPS and QUIC clients.

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:46.630
Now, on our basic tests,
QUIC saw a 1.3x improvement

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:50.110
in overall performance versus
the standard HTTPS on the load

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:53.075
balancer, which is pretty nice
for just setting one flag.

00:01:53.075 --> 00:01:55.450
If you're interested to know
more about the QUIC protocol

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:57.460
on GCP, then check
out the article linked

00:01:57.460 --> 00:01:58.970
in the description below.

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:01.540
And don't forget to subscribe
to our YouTube channel for more

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:03.609
great Cloud Performance
Atlas content

00:02:03.609 --> 00:02:05.150
because when it
comes to performance,

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:06.191
every millisecond counts.

00:02:06.191 --> 00:02:08.850
